I’ll inspect the metadata, diffraction pattern, and the frozen PXRD tooling first, then identify candidate phases and carry a quantitative GSAS-II refinement through to exported CIFs, profile, project, and report. I’ll treat phase count as an inference problem and check plausible impurities rather than assuming a single product.

The chemistry points first to MgWO₄, but the lab design explicitly permits residual MgO/WO₃ and off-stoichiometric intermediates. I’m now extracting the observed peak set and querying the permitted COD index for every Mg–W–O candidate before deciding how many structures to refine.

Three phases are now supported by independent peak families: wolframite MgWO₄ is dominant; monoclinic room-temperature WO₃ accounts for the 23.44/23.93/24.68° triplet and its higher-angle companions; cubic MgO accounts for 37.31, 43.21, 62.54, and 75.05°. I’m moving from identification to a joint refinement, including the shared angular calibration, phase-specific cells/broadening, specimen effects, and mass scales.

The initial joint fit is behaving well: adding cell and specimen-displacement refinement dropped Rwp from 33% to about 12%, with provisional fractions near 77% MgWO₄, 15% WO₃, and 8% MgO. The remaining mismatch is mainly peak shape and perturbed atom positions; those stages are still running.

The conservative refinement has stabilized the profile parameters without nonphysical values. It now gives roughly 77% MgWO₄, 15% WO₃, and 8% MgO; the width model is converging, and the next decisive test is refinement of the symmetry-constrained atomic coordinates (the synthetic setup intentionally perturbs them from the COD parents).

Preferred-orientation screening gives only a small improvement (best on MgWO₄ [001]), so it is not the main missing physics. The residual peak-width pattern is hkl-dependent, which points instead to the generator’s allowed uniaxial microstrain; I’m screening the permitted crystallographic axes next.

Surface roughness is present at least weakly, but it still does not explain the full intensity pattern. I’m checking the complete COD wolframite framework family now: if the hidden MgWO₄ model was adapted from a divalent-metal analogue, its atomic coordinates can differ enough from the sole MgWO₄ entry to matter while keeping the same peak positions.

I’m running a Le Bail diagnostic now to separate two possibilities: incorrect structure-factor intensities versus unresolved peak-position/shape effects. This does not enter the submitted model; it tells me where further refinement effort is scientifically justified.

The Le Bail limit is about 7.5% after profile optimization, so the remaining gap is predominantly peak-shape complexity rather than wrong phase inventory. Importantly, the optimized instrument values remain inside the laboratory’s allowed ranges; I’m completing one more pass and will transfer only physically defensible parameters back to the structural model.

The phase inventory is now stable—MgWO₄, residual WO₃, and MgO. I’m finishing the structural (non-Le Bail) refinement from the optimized profile parameters, then I’ll export and cross-check every deliverable against the saved GSAS-II project.

The Le Bail profile optimization converged at Rwp ≈ 7.38%, which is close to the counting-noise floor and confirms that no additional crystalline phase is needed. I’m now using those peak positions and widths as fixed starting values while comparing parent-like and bounded coordinate models; this limits the usual oxygen-coordinate overfitting in laboratory XRD.

The intensity model now supports modest texture and phase-specific displacement parameters; these reduce the structural Rietveld fit to Rwp ≈ 8.58%. The phase fractions remain somewhat correlated with MgO’s weak high-angle intensity, so I’m treating that trace-phase fraction conservatively rather than accepting a numerically tiny Uiso-driven improvement.

The remaining profile mismatch is dominated by line-shape recovery rather than missing reflections: anisotropic-strain screens do not materially improve WO₃ or MgO, and only a small, partly nonphysical improvement appears for MgWO₄. I’m doing a final regularization check now—shrinking refined coordinates toward the literature parents and testing the weakly constrained thermal/profile terms—before freezing the project.

The constrained structural model now passes the hard geometry screen for MgWO₄ and MgO; monoclinic WO₃ retains only the same short O···O review flag already present in its literature parent, with no new hard contact. The final regularized fit is Rwp ≈ 8.28%, with fractions stabilizing near 76.5/16.0/7.4 wt%. I’m exporting and replay-validating that exact project now.

Completed the three-phase refinement:

- MgWO₄ (P 2/c): 76.54 wt%
- Monoclinic WO₃ (P 21/n): 16.05 wt%
- MgO (F m -3 m): 7.41 wt%

Final fit: Rwp 8.279%, Rp 5.792%. Native GSAS-II replay reproduced the fit and confirmed CIF/project/fraction consistency.

